<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        carddemo-adapter-db — OPTIONAL outer-ring repository adapter of the
        hexagonal architecture (AAP §0.3.6).

        ============================================================
        EMPTY BY DEFAULT — this is INTENTIONAL, NOT A DEFECT.
        ============================================================
        Per AAP §0.1.1 (architectural override: "file-based batch processing
        by default (no PostgreSQL)") and §0.7.5 TODO resolution ("COBOL source
        uses VSAM KSDS with fixed-width records; default path is java.nio.file
        with fixed-width readers. JDBC adapter is empty and conditional"), this
        module is reserved for a future DB2/relational adapter if and only if
        the existing COBOL source contains embedded SQL. The COBOL source
        under ../app/cbl/ does NOT contain embedded SQL (verified by
        `grep -l "EXEC SQL" ../app/cbl/*.cbl` returning empty); therefore the
        default state of this module is empty.

        The DEFAULT outer-ring adapter is carddemo-adapter-file. This module
        (carddemo-adapter-db) exists ONLY to satisfy the hexagonal architecture
        contract (AAP §0.3.6): the carddemo-domain.port interfaces can be
        implemented by EITHER carddemo-adapter-file (default, populated) OR
        carddemo-adapter-db (optional, empty by default). The composition root
        in carddemo-app selects between adapters at startup via constructor
        injection per AAP §0.3.6.

        Why declare this module at all?
          1. To document the hexagonal architecture contract — port interfaces
             from carddemo-domain.port (AccountRepository, CardRepository,
             CardXrefRepository, CustomerRepository, TransactionRepository,
             DailyTransactionRepository, DiscountGroupRepository,
             TransactionCategoryBalanceRepository, TransactionTypeRepository,
             TransactionCategoryRepository, UserSecurityRepository — AAP §0.3.1)
             CAN be implemented by JDBC if needed.
          2. To provide a place for future JDBC implementations (e.g.,
             JdbcAccountRepository, JdbcCardRepository, etc.) without
             restructuring the project.
          3. To preserve the parent POM's <modules> list integrity (the parent
             already declares this module as its fourth child).

        Architectural rule (AAP §0.3.6, §0.6.12):
          * This module depends ONLY on carddemo-domain (innermost ring).
          * This module is depended on by carddemo-app (composition root) only
            when the JDBC adapter is selected at startup — never the reverse.
          * Outer rings of the hexagon must not be coupled to each other, so
            this module must NOT depend on carddemo-application,
            carddemo-batch, carddemo-adapter-file, carddemo-app, or
            carddemo-tests.

        Future contributor guidance (when adding a JDBC implementation):
          * Add the JDBC driver dependency with <scope>provided</scope> per
            AAP §0.4.1 — the driver is supplied at runtime by the
            carddemo-app composition root, not bundled into this adapter.
          * Update ../application.properties.example to document the JDBC
            connection properties (url, user, password, driver-class-name).
          * Add a section to ../MIGRATION_NOTES.md documenting why JDBC was
            needed and which COBOL programs triggered the decision.
          * Implement the port interfaces from carddemo-domain.port — each
            JdbcXxxRepository must produce byte-for-byte identical output to
            the corresponding FileXxxRepository per AAP §0.1.3 byte-for-byte
            file fidelity mandate.
          * Use only finalized Java 25 features (no preview flags) per AAP
            §0.6.7 and §0.7.4.

        Forbidden in this module (AAP §0.1.1 architectural override, §0.5.1,
        §0.6.7, §0.6.12, §0.7.4):
          * Concrete JDBC drivers by default (com.ibm.db2:jcc,
            org.postgresql:postgresql, com.oracle.database.jdbc:ojdbc11,
            com.mysql:mysql-connector-j, com.h2database:h2,
            org.mariadb.jdbc:mariadb-java-client, org.xerial:sqlite-jdbc).
            If added later, MUST use <scope>provided</scope>.
          * Connection pools (com.zaxxer:HikariCP, c3p0, commons-dbcp,
            commons-dbcp2, tomcat-jdbc) — none by default.
          * Hibernate / JPA / jakarta.persistence / javax.persistence — the
            domain layer is plain records, not entities.
          * Spring / Spring Boot / Spring Data JDBC / Spring Data JPA /
            Spring JDBC / Spring Batch / Spring Security / Spring MVC /
            Spring Cloud AWS — entire framework family forbidden by AAP §0.6.12.
          * MyBatis (org.mybatis:mybatis), jOOQ (org.jooq:jooq) — plain JDBC
            with PreparedStatement is the intended path if this module is
            ever populated.
          * Database migration tools — Flyway (org.flywaydb:flyway-core),
            Liquibase (org.liquibase:liquibase-core). Schema migration is
            out of scope per AAP §0.2.2.
          * Testcontainers (org.testcontainers:*), LocalStack — integration
            testing infrastructure is out of scope.
          * Lombok (org.projectlombok:lombok) — Java 25 records replace it.
          * Apache Commons IO, Guava — java.nio.file and java.util cover all
            needs per AAP §0.5.1.
          * Micrometer, Prometheus, OpenTelemetry, Jaeger — observability
            is JFR-only per AAP §0.6.12.
          * jqwik — property tests live in carddemo-tests per AAP §0.3.1.
          * The "enable-preview" JVM flag (AAP §0.7.4 — finalized JEPs only:
            JEP 506 ScopedValue, JEP 510 KDF, JEP 511 Module Import,
            JEP 513 Flexible Constructor Bodies, JEP 519 Compact Headers,
            JEP 521 Generational Shenandoah).
          * <source>/<target> tags — the parent POM's <release>25</release>
            is the single source of truth for the Java language level.
    -->

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.blitzy.carddemo</groupId>
        <artifactId>carddemo-parent</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>carddemo-adapter-db</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>CardDemo Adapter (DB / JDBC) — Optional, Empty by Default</name>
    <description>
        OPTIONAL JDBC adapter reserved for a future DB2/relational adapter
        implementation. EMPTY BY DEFAULT per AAP §0.7.5: the existing COBOL
        source under ../app/cbl/ uses VSAM KSDS exclusively (no EXEC SQL), so
        the default outer-ring repository adapter is carddemo-adapter-file
        (java.nio.file). This module exists ONLY to satisfy the hexagonal
        architecture contract — the carddemo-domain.port interfaces can be
        implemented by EITHER carddemo-adapter-file (default) OR
        carddemo-adapter-db (optional). The composition root in carddemo-app
        selects between adapters at startup via constructor injection. NO
        concrete JDBC driver is declared as a dependency by default; if added
        in the future, it MUST use scope=provided per AAP §0.4.1. NO Hibernate,
        NO JPA, NO Spring Data, NO Flyway, NO HikariCP — see AAP §0.6.12
        architectural override. Depends only on carddemo-domain for the port
        interfaces.
    </description>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
            Internal dependency: carddemo-domain.
            Provides the repository port interfaces (AccountRepository,
            CardRepository, CardXrefRepository, CustomerRepository,
            TransactionRepository, DailyTransactionRepository,
            DiscountGroupRepository, TransactionCategoryBalanceRepository,
            TransactionTypeRepository, TransactionCategoryRepository,
            UserSecurityRepository — AAP §0.3.1) and the domain records they
            exchange (AccountRecord, CardRecord, TranRecord, etc. — AAP §0.6.9)
            that any future JDBC implementation in this module will use.

            Although this module is EMPTY BY DEFAULT per AAP §0.7.5, declaring
            this dependency now ensures:
              (a) any future JDBC implementations can compile against the
                  port interfaces and the domain records they exchange,
              (b) the hexagonal architecture contract is explicitly documented
                  in the POM (per AAP §0.3.6 diagram), and
              (c) the Maven reactor correctly orders carddemo-domain before
                  this module at build time.

            Version inherited from the parent POM's dependencyManagement
            (${project.version} = 1.0.0-SNAPSHOT per AAP §0.5.1).
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.blitzy.carddemo</groupId>
            <artifactId>carddemo-domain</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            Production dependency: SLF4J API only (compile scope).
            Declared as a forward-looking dependency for any future JDBC
            repository implementations in this optional adapter module.
            Although this module is EMPTY BY DEFAULT per AAP §0.7.5 (the COBOL
            source contains no EXEC SQL so file-based persistence via
            carddemo-adapter-file is the default), the SLF4J facade is
            declared now so that when a future JDBC implementation (e.g.,
            JdbcAccountRepository) is added, it can immediately log
            connection/query/transaction events, SQLState code translations
            into the FileStatus sealed hierarchy, and JDBC driver registration
            warnings without further POM changes.

            The runtime implementation (logback-classic) is supplied at runtime
            by the composition root (carddemo-app); this adapter depends only
            on the API, keeping it free of binding to a specific logging
            backend per AAP §0.6.12.

            Version inherited from the parent POM's dependencyManagement
            (slf4j-api 2.0.16 per AAP §0.5.1).
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            Test dependencies (test scope only) — present to support future
            unit tests for JDBC repository implementations. Although the
            module is EMPTY BY DEFAULT per AAP §0.7.5, declaring the test
            infrastructure now ensures that any future JDBC implementation
            classes added to this module's src/test/java tree can be
            unit-tested without requiring further POM changes.

            Versions inherited from the parent POM's dependencyManagement:
              * junit-jupiter            via junit-bom 5.13.1 (AAP §0.5.1) —
                                                 unit tests for future JDBC
                                                 repository implementations
                                                 will use this aggregator.
              * junit-platform-launcher  1.13.1 — required minimum for JUnit
                                                 Platform native discovery by
                                                 maven-surefire-plugin 3.5.2;
                                                 aligns with jqwik 1.9.3's
                                                 minimum platform requirement
                                                 used across the reactor by
                                                 carddemo-tests (AAP §0.5.1).
              * assertj-core             3.26.3 — fluent assertions used to
                                                 express expectations such as
                                                 SQL result-set parsing
                                                 fidelity, JDBC PreparedStatement
                                                 parameter binding correctness,
                                                 SQLState code translation into
                                                 the FileStatus sealed hierarchy,
                                                 and BigDecimal scale
                                                 preservation across JDBC
                                                 TYPE.NUMERIC mappings
                                                 (AAP §0.5.1).
            Note: jqwik is NOT declared here — property-based tests for the
            Decimals utility and any cross-module property tests live in
            carddemo-tests per AAP §0.3.1.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-platform-launcher</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
            <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            ============================================================
            JDBC DRIVER PLACEHOLDER — INTENTIONALLY ABSENT BY DEFAULT
            ============================================================
            Per AAP §0.4.1, §0.5.1, §0.6.12, and §0.7.5:
              * The COBOL source contains NO embedded SQL (verified:
                `grep -l "EXEC SQL" ../app/cbl/*.cbl` returns empty).
              * The default persistence path is file-based
                (carddemo-adapter-file via java.nio.file).
              * This module is EMPTY BY DEFAULT.
              * If a concrete JDBC driver is added in the future (DB2,
                PostgreSQL, Oracle, etc.), it MUST be declared with
                <scope>provided</scope> per AAP §0.4.1 so the host
                application (carddemo-app shaded jar) provides the driver
                at runtime — keeping the adapter free of binding to a
                specific database vendor.

            DO NOT add any of the following by default (forbidden until
            embedded SQL is discovered in app/cbl/):
              * com.ibm.db2:jcc                            — DB2 LUW / z/OS
              * org.postgresql:postgresql                  — PostgreSQL
              * com.oracle.database.jdbc:ojdbc11           — Oracle 21c+
              * com.mysql:mysql-connector-j                — MySQL 8+
              * mysql:mysql-connector-java                 — MySQL 5.x/8.x legacy
              * com.h2database:h2                          — H2 (embedded)
              * org.mariadb.jdbc:mariadb-java-client       — MariaDB
              * org.xerial:sqlite-jdbc                     — SQLite

            DO NOT add a connection pool by default:
              * com.zaxxer:HikariCP
              * c3p0:c3p0  /  com.mchange:c3p0
              * commons-dbcp:commons-dbcp
              * org.apache.commons:commons-dbcp2
              * org.apache.tomcat:tomcat-jdbc

            DO NOT add ORM, JPA, persistence frameworks, or migration tools:
              * org.hibernate.*                            — Hibernate ORM
              * jakarta.persistence:jakarta.persistence-api
              * javax.persistence:javax.persistence-api
              * org.springframework.data:spring-data-jdbc
              * org.springframework.data:spring-data-jpa
              * org.springframework:spring-jdbc
              * org.mybatis:mybatis
              * org.jooq:jooq
              * org.flywaydb:flyway-core
              * org.liquibase:liquibase-core
            ============================================================
        -->
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
                maven-compiler-plugin — activated for this module so the
                parent's pluginManagement (version 3.13.0,
                <release>25</release>, no preview flag per AAP §0.7.4,
                -parameters for record component names) applies during the
                compile phase. The Java language level is set ONCE in the
                parent POM; this child must not redeclare it. Although the
                module is EMPTY BY DEFAULT per AAP §0.7.5, declaring the
                plugin keeps the compile lifecycle wired correctly for any
                future production source files (e.g., JdbcAccountRepository,
                JdbcCardRepository, etc.) added to src/main/java.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

            <!--
                maven-surefire-plugin — activated for this module so the
                parent's pluginManagement (version 3.5.2, JUnit Platform
                native discovery, no "enable-preview" argLine per AAP §0.7.4)
                applies during the test phase. Test classes are discovered by
                the standard **/*Test.java and **/*Tests.java patterns.
                Required so that any future JDBC unit tests added to this
                module's src/test/java tree are discovered and executed
                during `mvn test`.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
